---
import Layout from '../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
import ChessBoard from '../components/ChessBoard.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="About Me"
	description="About Bekesta"
	pubDate={new Date('August 08 2026')}
>
	 <ChessBoard
		  fen="4kb1r/p2n1ppp/4q3/4p1B1/4P3/1Q6/PPP2PPP/2KR4 w k - 0 16"
		 boardSize="320px"
	 />

	 <p>
		  My father taught me to play chess when I was a child. I remember he played 1. e4 and played
		  what I later learned was the Ruy Lopez. Sometimes he played the King's Gambit. He beat me easily.
		  Until one day when I beat him. It was a great feeling. 
	 </p>

	 <p>
		  Aside from the occasional games with my father, I didn't play chess until some time in my early
		  twenties (around 1986) when I joined a chess club near the US Navy base at which I was stationed. 
		  Unfortunately, the chess club closed shortly after I started and that was the end of chess.
	 </p>

	 <p>
		  After the Navy, I played a little informal speed chess with a work colleague. Intending to get better, I collected a number of chess books I never 
		  read. Except I did read a book by Reuben Fine called "The Middle Game in Chess". Since I didn't
		  really play, I never got the chance to put what I learned into practice and I don't remember much
		  about the book. I remember I really enjoyed going through the 2 chapters related to mating attacks
		  and attacks on the king. 
	 </p>

	 <p>
		  Through the decades I never got rid of those books, always thinking I'd pick up chess again and
		  read them. About 3 years ago my wife and I were cleaning out our basement. We were trying to be
		  serious about reducing clutter. She put the question to me: "Are you really ever going to read 
		  these books? You've had them for decades". To the Goodwill they went.
	 </p>

	 <p>
		  Unbeknownst to me, one of my sons had signed up at chess.com and one year ago, in August 2025,
		  he challenged me to a game of chess. I got an account at chess.com and we played (beat him easily). 
		  I quickly got hooked. I was 62 years old and recently retired and thought I finally had time to devote
		  to playing chess. I just wished I had those books I threw out 2 years prior.
	 </p>

	 <p>
		  I wasted a lot of time playing blitz games before deciding I needed to take a more disciplined
		  approach and try to improve. One year after starting at chess.com, my Rapid rating was 1350 over 255 
		  games. But blitz wasn't the only trap I had fallen in to. I spent 
		  a lot of time watching YouTube chess videos, got more chess courses on chessable that I can ever watch, 
		  and jumped from chess resource to chess resource.
	 </p>

	 <p>
		  I learned about <a href="lichess.org">lichess.org</a> and created my bekesta account there, intending it to be a reset account
		  where I played "serious" chess, which didn't really happen.
	 </p>

	 <p>
		  Then last month I signed up for Noël Studer's <a href="https://nextlevelchess.com/simplified-chess-improvement-system/">Simplified Chess Improvement System</a>.
	 </p>

	 <p>
		  I'm going to make a serious attempt at keeping a simple chess training program and see where it takes
		  me over the course of one year. I will be using my <a href="https://lichess.org/@/bekesta">Lichess.org bekesta</a> account for all 
		  my chess improvement work. I'll still probably play some fun games on my chess.com account, but
		  I will try to keep that to a minimum.
	 </p>
</Layout>
